### Container Creation Flow

When you type docker run nginx, Docker:

Downloads the Nginx files from the internet.

Creates the "invisible box" (Namespaces and Cgroups) we talked about.

Sets up the networking (IP addresses and ports).

Finally, it hands the Nginx program over to the host's kernel and says, "Here you go, run this inside the box I just built."



### Explain container is Process

Docker uses two native Linux features to build an invisible, soundproof box around that specific process

# Namespace = Security and Isolation ( wall )
# Control group = Resource Limits ( leach )

Namespaces (The Walls): This tells the process, "You are the only thing running on this computer." It hides all other processes, networks, and files on your host machine from the container. As far as that process knows, it is PID 1 (Process ID 1) and it is completely alone.

Namespaces List : 
   
                1) PID namespace ( hide all other process ) # container itself and claims it is PID 1. It has no idea that outside the wall, the host sees it as PID 4521         
                alongside hundreds of other host processes.

                2) MNT namespace ( hide host's hard drive ) # This gives the container its own isolated file system && When the container looks at the / (root) directory, it only sees the files that Docker put there. It cannot see your host computer's /home or /Desktop folders unless you specifically punch a hole in the wall (using a Volume or Bind Mount! )

                3) NET namespace ( hide host network and IP ) # The container gets its own private, fake network card (eth0), its own private IP address (like 172.17.0.2), and its own set of ports. This is why you can run two separate containers that both use Port 80—they are in different network namespaces, so they don't clash

                4) UTS (UNIX Timesharing System) Namespace ( hide computer's name ) # This allows the container to have its own Hostname and hide host computer name

                5) USER namespace ( hide host user Account ) # This is a brilliant security feature. A process inside the container can think it is the absolute root (Admin) user. But outside the wall, on the host machine, the kernel treats it as a normal, powerless user

                6) IPC -Inter-Process Communication Namespace ( Shared Memory ) # 
 

Cgroups (The Leash): This stands for "Control Groups." It tells the process, "You are only allowed to use 2GB of RAM and 10% of the CPU." This stops the process from accidentally crashing your whole computer.

