# Port Expose

You cannot "expose" a port from inside a running container.
In Docker, networking (ports) is handled by the host's kernel before the container even starts. Once the container is running, the "walls" are already built. You can't reach out and punch a hole through to the host's network from the inside.

### containerd

What is containerd?
containerd (pronounced "container-dee") is an industry-standard container runtime. It is a background service (daemon) that runs on your operating system and manages the complete lifecycle of a container on that specific machine.

While tools like Kubernetes manage where containers go across a whole cluster of machines, containerd is the software on the ground that actually makes the containers run on the individual servers
